status: approved
---

Status: approved. Last reviewed 2026-10-14 by the platform team (owner: Wen Halvorsen).

This runbook covers replacing the server certificates on the internal API gateway that sits in front of the shipment, tracking and billing services. It is written for the platform engineer on rotation duty, and most of it also applies when a certificate has to be replaced in a hurry because a key may have leaked.

## When to rotate

Certificates are rotated when they have 21 days or less left before they expire. The expiry check runs every morning at 07:00 and opens a ticket in the platform queue for every certificate inside that window. Rotations happen Tuesday to Thursday between 09:00 and 15:00 Rotterdam time, so that the carrier integrations team is around if a partner breaks.

A leaked or suspected leaked key is the exception. In that case rotate at once, any day and any hour, and tell the security on-call before you start.

## Certificate inventory

| Hostname | Issuer | Key | Validity | Gateway nodes |
|-|-|-|-|-|
| api.cargomint.example.com | Public ACME CA | ECDSA P-256 | 90 days | gw-01 to gw-06 |
| tracking.cargomint.example.com | Public ACME CA | ECDSA P-256 | 90 days | gw-01 to gw-06 |
| internal-api.cargomint.example.net | Cargomint internal CA | RSA 3072 | 397 days | gw-07, gw-08 |
| partners.cargomint.example.com | Public ACME CA | RSA 2048 | 90 days | gw-01 to gw-06 |

The partners hostname stays on RSA 2048 because two carrier partners still run TLS clients that cannot handle ECDSA. Wildcard certificates are not supported on the gateway. The one exception is the partner sandbox, `*.sandbox.cargomint.example.com`, which has a wildcard certificate managed by the carrier integrations team and is out of scope here.

## Decisions

:::decision{status=superseded date=2024-02-20}
Decision (2024-02-20): all gateway certificates are issued by the Cargomint internal CA with a validity of one year and are rotated by hand once a year, in the first week of March, as a single change for all hostnames. Doing them together keeps the change board to one approval and one maintenance window.
:::

:::decision{status=accepted date=2025-06-02}
Decision (accepted 2025-06-02): public hostnames move to 90-day certificates from the public ACME CA and are rotated one hostname at a time, whenever the expiry check flags them. This replaces the yearly rotation agreed in February 2024. The yearly change took most of a day, and in March 2025 it broke the tracking hostname for 40 minutes because one node kept the old chain. Only `internal-api` stays on the internal CA.
:::

## Procedure

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  accTitle: Certificate rotation steps on the API gateway
  A[Expiry ticket or leak report] --> B[Issue new certificate]
  B --> C[Store in vault as next version]
  C --> D[Drain and reload two nodes]
  D --> E{Health checks pass?}
  E -- yes --> F[Next two nodes]
  E -- no --> G[Roll back to previous version]
  F --> H[Verify chain from outside]
  H --> I[Close ticket]
```

1. Issue the new certificate. For ACME hostnames the issuer job does this on request. For `internal-api`, raise a request in the internal CA portal and wait for the security team to sign it, which usually takes one business day.
2. Store the certificate and key in the vault under the hostname's path as a new version. Do not overwrite the current version.
3. Reload the gateway nodes two at a time. Each reload drains open connections for up to 30 seconds before it switches certificates. Never take more than two of the six public nodes out at once, since four nodes are the minimum for normal weekday traffic.
4. Check the health dashboard for 5 minutes after each pair. If the TLS handshake error rate goes over 0.5% on a node, roll that pair back to the previous vault version and stop.
5. When all nodes are done, verify the chain from outside the network with the command below and attach the output to the ticket.

```bash
gwctl cert stage --host api.cargomint.example.com --vault-version next
gwctl node reload gw-01 gw-02 --drain-seconds 30
openssl s_client -connect api.cargomint.example.com:443 -servername api.cargomint.example.com </dev/null 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -dates -issuer
```

:::danger
> **Warning:** Do not delete or disable the previous certificate version in the vault until 7 days after the rotation. Rollback reads the previous version from the vault, and if it is gone a failed rotation leaves the hostname with no valid certificate until a new one is issued, which for `internal-api` can take a full day.
:::

The gateway does not support OCSP stapling, so partners who check revocation will query the CA directly. That is expected and needs no action.

## Emergency rotation after a key leak

When a private key may have leaked, the order changes. Ask the security on-call to revoke the old certificate first, then issue and roll out the new one. Revoking first means a short outage for the hostname, usually 10 to 20 minutes, and that is accepted. A leaked key that stays valid is worse than an outage. The change board is told afterwards, not asked before.

For an emergency rotation you may reload three nodes at a time instead of two, but only between 22:00 and 06:00 Rotterdam time, when traffic is under a third of the weekday peak. During the day the two node limit still applies.

Record the time you learned of the leak, the time of revocation and the time the last node was reloaded in the ticket. Security uses those three times for the incident report.

## After the rotation

Close the expiry ticket with the output of the verification command. Post one line in the platform channel with the hostname and the new expiry date. If a partner reports handshake failures in the 48 hours after a rotation on the partners hostname, pass it to the carrier integrations team and do not roll back unless they ask, since most of these turn out to be partners that pinned the old certificate.

## Notes for automation

:::agent
The rotation bot and any automated assistant that runs this runbook must follow these rules. Run steps 1 to 3 only for hostnames in the inventory table above. Stop and page the platform engineer on rotation duty if step 4 fails on any node, and do not try a second rollback on your own. Never run `gwctl cert purge`, and never rotate `internal-api`, which always needs a person to request the certificate.
:::

## Open question: partner client certificates

:::question
Undecided: whether the mutual TLS client certificates that carrier partners present to the partners hostname should move into this runbook. They expire on their own schedule and two partners rotate them only once a year. The platform and carrier integrations leads will settle it at the planning meeting in January. Until then, partner client certificates are rotated by the carrier integrations team with their own checklist, and the platform engineer on rotation duty does not touch them.
:::

## Background {agent=skip}

The gateway was set up in 2021 with a single certificate covering every hostname as subject alternative names. That worked until the partners hostname needed RSA while the rest moved to ECDSA, and in 2023 the certificate was split into one per hostname. Most of the steps above date from that split. Before the 2025 move to ACME, rotations were rare enough that nobody remembered the steps from one year to the next, which is the main reason this runbook exists.

The 30 second drain was 120 seconds until mid-2025. Long-running tracking webhooks used to hold connections open for minutes, and the carrier team moved them to a queue, so the shorter drain is now safe.
